<!doctype html><html><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>The High-Performance Leadership System</title><style>@page{size:Letter;margin:.42in}.page{page-break-after:always;break-after:page;position:relative;min-height:9.85in;box-sizing:border-box;padding:.06in .08in .28in}.page:last-child{page-break-after:auto}.cover{background:linear-gradient(135deg,#081c2c,#0d5d68 60%,#d2a247);color:white;padding:.6in}.eyebrow{font:700 9px Arial;letter-spacing:1.8px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#d2a247;margin-bottom:15px}h1{font:700 31px Georgia;margin:0 0 12px;line-height:1.05}h2{font:700 20px Georgia;color:#093a4d;border-bottom:3px solid #d2a247;padding-bottom:5px;margin:0 0 10px}h3{font:700 12px Arial;color:#0d5d68;margin:10px 0 4px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.4px}p,li{font:10.1px/1.35 Arial;color:#1e2c35;margin:0 0 6px}.cover p{color:#e5f0ef;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;max-width:5.7in}.kicker{font:700 14px Arial;letter-spacing:1px;color:#d2a247;margin-top:1.6in}.grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:13px}.card{border:1px solid #b8c9cc;border-left:5px solid #0d5d68;padding:8px;background:#f7faf9}.gold{border-left-color:#d2a247;background:#fffaf0}.callout{background:#e7f2f1;border-left:5px solid #0d5d68;padding:9px;margin:8px 0}.quote{font:italic 11px/1.4 Georgia;color:#093a4d}.small{font-size:8.7px}.tiny{font-size:7.9px}.two{columns:2;column-gap:18px}.two p,.two ul{break-inside:avoid}table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:6px 0 9px;font:8.7px/1.25 Arial}th{background:#093a4d;color:#fff;text-align:left;padding:5px}td{vertical-align:top;border:1px solid #b8c9cc;padding:5px}tr:nth-child(even){background:#f3f7f6}.diagram{background:#f7faf9;border:1px solid #b8c9cc;padding:9px;margin:8px 0}.bar{height:13px;background:#dce8e6;margin:4px 0;position:relative}.bar span{display:block;height:13px;background:#0d5d68;color:white;font:8px Arial;padding-left:4px;box-sizing:border-box}.line{border-bottom:1px solid #8ea5a7;height:15px;margin:4px 0}.footer{position:absolute;bottom:.04in;left:.08in;right:.08in;border-top:1px solid #b8c9cc;padding-top:4px;font:7.8px Arial;color:#5a6c70;display:flex;justify-content:space-between}.badge{display:inline-block;background:#d2a247;color:#14242b;font:700 8px Arial;padding:3px 6px;margin-right:4px}.check li{list-style:'✓  ';padding-left:3px}.exercise{background:#fffaf0;border:1px solid #d2a247;padding:8px;margin-top:8px}.label{font:700 8px Arial;letter-spacing:1px;color:#876418;text-transform:uppercase}</style><style>.page{page-break-after:auto!important;break-after:auto!important;min-height:auto!important;padding:.06in .08in .10in!important;margin:0 0 .10in!important}.footer{display:none!important}h2{break-after:avoid-page}table,.card,.callout,.exercise,.diagram{break-inside:avoid}</style></head><body><section class="page cover"><div class="eyebrow" style="color:#d2a247">FACILITATOR GUIDE · LEARNER WORKBOOK · BUYER TOOLKIT</div><div class="kicker">RAISE STANDARDS · BUILD TRUST · CREATE OWNERSHIP</div><h1>The High-Performance<br>Leadership System</h1><p>A practical operating system for frontline leaders who need reliable execution without fear, burnout, or dependence on heroics.</p><div style="margin-top:1.2in"><span class="badge">1-DAY WORKSHOP</span><span class="badge">10-VIDEO SERIES</span><span class="badge">90-DAY REINFORCEMENT</span></div><p style="margin-top:1.3in;font-size:10px">Built for newly promoted managers, team leads, and organizations ready to turn strategy into observable daily behavior.</p></section><section class="page"><div class="eyebrow">THE HIGH-PERFORMANCE LEADERSHIP SYSTEM</div><h2>Buyer Executive Brief</h2><div class="grid"><div><h3>The buyer problem</h3><p>Inconsistent execution is rarely solved by another dashboard or a louder escalation. It is usually a management-system problem: standards are vague, authority is unclear, feedback arrives late, and managers either rescue the work or avoid the hard conversation.</p><p>This product gives leaders a shared language and a repeatable sequence: specify the outcome, create commitment, coach early, follow through fairly, and install routines that keep good work visible.</p><div class="callout"><b>Commercial promise.</b> Participants leave with tools they can use in their next 1:1, team meeting, delegation brief, and performance correction.</div></div><div><h3>Best-fit buyers</h3><ul class="check"><li>New-manager populations with uneven coaching confidence</li><li>Service or operations teams where rework and escalation are rising</li><li>Growth-stage firms outgrowing founder-led problem solving</li><li>Organizations seeking a common management language</li></ul><h3>Outcomes to measure</h3><p>Clarity of expectations, on-time delivery, quality or rework, escalations, employee confidence, manager practice completion, and relevant customer measures.</p></div></div><table><tr><th>Offer</th><th>What is included</th><th>Buyer result</th></tr><tr><td>Essentials</td><td>10 videos, workbook, self-assessment, discussion guide</td><td>A scalable common language</td></tr><tr><td>Team rollout</td><td>Essentials + live practice labs + facilitation</td><td>Managers rehearse the hard conversations</td></tr><tr><td>Transformation</td><td>Team rollout + baseline, coaching circles, 90-day measurement</td><td>Behavior change tied to operating metrics</td></tr></table><div class="exercise"><div class="label">Buyer discovery question</div><p>“Where does performance currently depend on a manager noticing, rescuing, or escalating work that the team should be able to own?” Use the answer to select the first team friction point.</p></div><span class="small">Evidence base: Edmondson (1999); Locke & Latham (2002); Hattie & Timperley (2007); Deci, Olafsen & Ryan (2017); Gallup (2024). Full references at the end.</span></section><section class="page"><div class="eyebrow">THE HIGH-PERFORMANCE LEADERSHIP SYSTEM</div><h2>The Operating Premise: Standards × Warmth</h2><p>High performance is not a tradeoff between demanding results and treating people well. Sustainable performance requires both. <b>Standards</b> make outcomes, quality, timing, decision rights, and consequences explicit. <b>Warmth</b> means respect, curiosity, fairness, recognition, support, and a credible belief that people can succeed.</p><div class="diagram"><table><tr><th></th><th>LOW WARMTH</th><th>HIGH WARMTH</th></tr><tr><th>HIGH STANDARDS</th><td><b>Directive pressure</b><br>Short-term compliance; hiding problems; fear of bad news.</td><td><b>High-performance leadership</b><br>Clear commitments; early feedback; ownership; durable results.</td></tr><tr><th>LOW STANDARDS</th><td><b>Disengaged management</b><br>Confusion; low energy; drifting priorities.</td><td><b>Comfortable drift</b><br>Good intent; weak follow-through; uneven quality.</td></tr></table></div><div class="grid"><div class="card"><h3>Manager test</h3><p>Can each person state what good looks like, what they can decide, how progress is checked, and what to do when blocked?</p></div><div class="card gold"><h3>Team test</h3><p>Can someone surface a risk early without being blamed, and can the manager still hold the line on the agreed standard?</p></div></div><p class="quote">“High warmth is not lowering the bar. It is making success more possible and feedback more usable.”</p><span class="small">Evidence base: Edmondson (1999); Locke & Latham (2002); Hattie & Timperley (2007); Deci, Olafsen & Ryan (2017); Gallup (2024). Full references at the end.</span></section><section class="page"><div class="eyebrow">THE HIGH-PERFORMANCE LEADERSHIP SYSTEM</div><h2>Why This System Works: Research to Practice</h2><p>This program translates established evidence into manager behaviors. It does not present research as decoration: each finding changes a conversation, routine, or measurement practice.</p><table><tr><th>Research insight</th><th>Practical implication</th><th>System behavior</th></tr><tr><td>Specific, challenging goals improve performance when feedback and commitment are present (Locke & Latham, 2002).</td><td>“Do your best” is insufficient. Standards must be observable.</td><td>Use CLEAR: Concrete outcome, Level of quality, Evidence, Authority, Rhythm.</td></tr><tr><td>Psychological safety supports speaking up and learning (Edmondson, 1999).</td><td>Managers need bad news early, not false reassurance.</td><td>Ask before telling; separate facts from labels; thank people for surfacing risk.</td></tr><tr><td>Feedback is strongest when it addresses task, process, and self-regulation rather than personality (Hattie & Timperley, 2007).</td><td>“Be more proactive” is weak feedback.</td><td>Use FACT: Facts, Ask, Clarify, Test the next action.</td></tr><tr><td>Autonomy, competence, and relatedness support internal motivation (Deci et al., 2017).</td><td>Ownership cannot be demanded by slogan.</td><td>Delegate outcomes with decision rights, guardrails, and coaching.</td></tr></table><div class="callout"><b>Facilitator note:</b> Ask participants to challenge the evidence against a real example. “What behavior would change on Monday if we took this seriously?” This prevents theory from becoming a compliance exercise.</div><div class="exercise"><div class="label">Reflection</div><p>Name one current management practice that produces short-term compliance but weak learning. What would you replace it with?</p><div class="line"></div><div class="line"></div></div></section><section class="page"><div class="eyebrow">THE HIGH-PERFORMANCE LEADERSHIP SYSTEM</div><h2>Diagnostic: Your Pressure Pattern</h2><p>Under pressure, managers tend to repeat a default pattern. The diagnostic is not a personality test. It is a prompt for choosing a more useful response before pressure makes the choice for you. Score each statement: 1 = rarely true, 3 = sometimes true, 5 = often true.</p><table><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Score 1–5</th><th>What a high score may signal</th></tr><tr><td>I take work back when a deadline is at risk.</td><td>____</td><td>Rescuing; weak ownership or unclear guardrails</td></tr><tr><td>I soften or postpone a difficult performance conversation.</td><td>____</td><td>Avoidance; warmth without standards</td></tr><tr><td>I become more directive and explain less.</td><td>____</td><td>Pressure; standards without curiosity</td></tr><tr><td>I assume people know what “good” means.</td><td>____</td><td>Ambiguous standards</td></tr><tr><td>I inspect activity but do not discuss decision rights.</td><td>____</td><td>Control rather than accountability</td></tr><tr><td>I recognize effort more often than specific behavior.</td><td>____</td><td>Weak reinforcement loop</td></tr><tr><td>I ask for updates but do not agree a next action.</td><td>____</td><td>Meetings without commitments</td></tr><tr><td>I only hear about a problem after it is urgent.</td><td>____</td><td>Low safety or weak operating rhythm</td></tr></table><div class="grid"><div class="card"><h3>Interpretation</h3><p>Circle your two highest items. They are your <b>pressure pattern</b>, not your identity. Identify the triggering condition, the cost to the team, and one replacement behavior.</p></div><div class="card gold"><h3>Pair debrief prompt</h3><p>“When I am under pressure, my team experiences me as ____. The standard I want to preserve is ____. The warmth behavior I will add is ____.”</p></div></div><div class="exercise"><div class="label">Commitment</div><p>In the next seven days, I will notice this trigger: ____________________ and replace my default response with: ____________________.</p></div></section><section class="page"><div class="eyebrow">THE HIGH-PERFORMANCE LEADERSHIP SYSTEM</div><h2>Module 1: The Cost of Unclear Leadership</h2><p><b>Purpose:</b> Create urgency without blaming managers. Teams do not become inconsistent because people lack effort alone. They become inconsistent when outcomes, ownership, feedback, and decision rights are fuzzy.</p><div class="two"><p><b>Teach:</b> Separate activity, output, and outcome. “Attend more client meetings” is activity. “Send a follow-up” is output. “Every qualified opportunity has a tailored next step within one business day” is an outcome standard that can be observed and improved.</p><p><b>Facilitation flow (25 minutes):</b> Open with a familiar friction: a customer waits, work returns for rework, or a decision travels through three people. Ask learners to locate the unclear element. Was the outcome unknown? Was the quality bar undefined? Was authority absent? Was follow-up missing?</p><p><b>Debrief:</b> Do not accept “they need to be more accountable” as a diagnosis. Ask, “Accountable for what, by when, with what evidence, and with what authority?”</p><p><b>Watch-out:</b> This is not an invitation to over-specify every task. The system clarifies the minimum needed for good judgment, then gives people room to act.</p></div><table><tr><th>Friction symptom</th><th>Likely system gap</th><th>Manager move tomorrow</th></tr><tr><td>Repeated rework</td><td>Quality standard is implicit</td><td>Show one strong and one acceptable example; agree evidence.</td></tr><tr><td>Late surprises</td><td>Risks are punished or escalation path is unclear</td><td>Ask for risks at a fixed weekly point; thank early disclosure.</td></tr><tr><td>Manager bottleneck</td><td>Authority sits with the manager</td><td>Name decisions the owner can make without approval.</td></tr><tr><td>Status meetings with no movement</td><td>No commitment or review rhythm</td><td>End each discussion with owner, action, date, and check-in.</td></tr></table><div class="exercise"><div class="label">Practice: 15-minute friction map</div><p>Choose one recurring friction. Describe the expected outcome, current behavior, cost to customer/capacity, and the manager action that would clarify, enable, or inspect it.</p></div></section><section class="page"><div class="eyebrow">THE HIGH-PERFORMANCE LEADERSHIP SYSTEM</div><h2>Worksheet: Performance Friction Map</h2><p>Use this worksheet on one real team problem. Work from observable evidence. The goal is not to assign blame; it is to find the leadership-system condition that makes the gap repeat.</p><table><tr><th>Prompt</th><th>Your working notes</th></tr><tr><td>1. What must be true when this work is done well?</td><td><br><br></td></tr><tr><td>2. What is happening instead? Include facts, dates, examples, or data.</td><td><br><br></td></tr><tr><td>3. What is the impact on customer, colleague, cost, risk, or capacity?</td><td><br><br></td></tr><tr><td>4. Which element is unclear: outcome, quality, evidence, authority, rhythm, skill, resource, or incentive?</td><td><br><br></td></tr><tr><td>5. What will the manager clarify, enable, inspect, or reinforce this week?</td><td><br><br></td></tr><tr><td>6. What measure or evidence will show progress by the next review?</td><td><br><br></td></tr></table><div class="callout"><b>Facilitator instruction:</b> In triads, give each person four minutes to describe the friction, four minutes for peers to ask only diagnostic questions, then four minutes to state a manager experiment. The listener must not solve the problem before the evidence is clear.</div><p class="quote">Useful diagnostic question: “If a capable new hire joined tomorrow, could they discover the expected standard and escalation path without reading the manager’s mind?”</p></section><section class="page"><div class="eyebrow">THE HIGH-PERFORMANCE LEADERSHIP SYSTEM</div><h2>Case Study 1: The Aurora Service Desk</h2><p><b>Situation.</b> Aurora’s 14-person service desk has a 91% first-response target. The target has slipped to 76% for two months. The manager, Priya, reviews the queue twice daily and often reassigns tickets herself. Her team says they are “doing their best,” while customers report inconsistent updates.</p><p><b>What you can observe.</b> A sample of 40 tickets shows that 18 lacked a next update date, 11 were assigned to specialists without a customer-facing owner, and 9 sat blocked for more than one day. Priya has not defined when agents can re-prioritize work or escalate resource conflicts. In meetings, she asks, “Can we all be more on top of this?”</p><table><tr><th>Stakeholder</th><th>What they say</th><th>What it may mean</th></tr><tr><td>Priya</td><td>“I have to jump in or customers wait.”</td><td>Rescuing temporarily protects the metric but hides the system gap.</td></tr><tr><td>Senior agent</td><td>“I do not want to step on anyone’s toes.”</td><td>Decision rights are unclear.</td></tr><tr><td>New agent</td><td>“I was waiting for the specialist.”</td><td>No standard for proactive customer update or blocked-work escalation.</td></tr><tr><td>Customer</td><td>“I can handle a delay; I cannot handle silence.”</td><td>Quality includes communication, not just resolution.</td></tr></table><div class="exercise"><div class="label">Team task</div><p>Diagnose the primary gap. Draft one CLEAR standard for ticket ownership. Identify one warmth behavior Priya must use so that early escalation becomes safe rather than punitive.</p></div></section><section class="page"><div class="eyebrow">THE HIGH-PERFORMANCE LEADERSHIP SYSTEM</div><h2>Case Study 1 Debrief: From Rescue to Ownership</h2><p><b>Diagnosis.</b> Aurora does not principally have an effort problem. It has a standard, authority, and rhythm problem. Priya’s rescue behavior teaches agents that ownership ends at the queue boundary. Her vague request for people to be “more on top of it” gives no executable agreement.</p><table><tr><th>CLEAR element</th><th>Aurora agreement</th></tr><tr><td>Concrete outcome</td><td>Every open customer ticket has a named customer-facing owner and a next update date.</td></tr><tr><td>Level of quality</td><td>Customers receive an accurate update within one business day, even when another team owns the technical fix.</td></tr><tr><td>Evidence</td><td>Queue audit checks owner and next-update fields at 10:00 and 15:00; weekly sample checks message quality.</td></tr><tr><td>Authority</td><td>Agents may re-prioritize within their tier and request specialist help; team lead resolves cross-tier conflict.</td></tr><tr><td>Rhythm</td><td>Blocked tickets are raised in the 15-minute daily risk review; unresolved blocks escalate after one business day.</td></tr></table><div class="grid"><div class="card"><h3>Priya’s new opening</h3><p>“We missed the response standard. I own the lack of clarity here. From today, each ticket has one customer owner and a next update date. What will make that difficult, and what authority do you need to make it work?”</p></div><div class="card gold"><h3>What not to do</h3><p>Do not announce a new rule, then continue privately reassigning tickets. That would keep the old system alive. Coach the owner in public routines; correct gaps privately and quickly.</p></div></div><p><b>Measure after 30 days:</b> first response, percent of tickets with an update date, blocked-ticket age, repeat contacts, and agent clarity pulse.</p></section><section class="page"><div class="eyebrow">THE HIGH-PERFORMANCE LEADERSHIP SYSTEM</div><h2>Module 2: Make Standards Executable with CLEAR</h2><p>A standard is executable when a person can act on it without guessing what the manager has in mind. CLEAR converts an aspiration into a visible agreement.</p><table><tr><th>Letter</th><th>Question the manager answers</th><th>Example: client follow-up</th></tr><tr><td>C · Concrete outcome</td><td>What must happen?</td><td>Every qualified opportunity receives a tailored follow-up.</td></tr><tr><td>L · Level of quality</td><td>What is acceptable, and what is excellent?</td><td>Reference the conversation and propose a relevant next step.</td></tr><tr><td>E · Evidence</td><td>How will we verify progress or completion?</td><td>Log next action and date in the CRM.</td></tr><tr><td>A · Authority</td><td>What decisions can the owner make?</td><td>Offer approved options; involve manager only for exceptions.</td></tr><tr><td>R · Rhythm</td><td>When do we check, and how do we escalate?</td><td>Within one business day; blocked decisions raised after two days.</td></tr></table><div class="callout"><b>Teaching narrative:</b> Clarity is an act of respect. It reduces hidden rules, protects fairness, and lets managers distinguish a skill gap from a motivation or resource problem. The objective is not surveillance. Evidence creates a shared reality from which the employee can learn and the manager can coach.</div><div class="exercise"><div class="label">Practice prompt</div><p>Rewrite: “Improve customer follow-up.” Then test it: could two reasonable people tell whether it happened, whether it was good enough, and what to do if blocked?</p></div><p class="small">Facilitator timing: 12-minute teach, 15-minute individual rewrite, 18-minute peer test, 10-minute harvest.</p></section><section class="page"><div class="eyebrow">THE HIGH-PERFORMANCE LEADERSHIP SYSTEM</div><h2>CLEAR Practice Lab: Weak to Workable</h2><p>Use the table to turn ambiguous manager language into an agreement that a capable person can execute. Add context, not bureaucracy.</p><table><tr><th>Weak expectation</th><th>Your CLEAR rewrite</th><th>Peer quality test</th></tr><tr><td>“Own the handover.”</td><td><br><br></td><td>Can we observe the outcome and name decision rights?</td></tr><tr><td>“Be more proactive.”</td><td><br><br></td><td>Does it describe a behavior rather than a trait?</td></tr><tr><td>“Improve quality.”</td><td><br><br></td><td>Is acceptable quality illustrated or evidenced?</td></tr><tr><td>“Keep stakeholders informed.”</td><td><br><br></td><td>Does it specify rhythm and escalation?</td></tr></table><div class="grid"><div class="card"><h3>Peer coach questions</h3><ul><li>What exactly will be different?</li><li>What evidence will prove it?</li><li>Where can the owner decide?</li><li>What risk warrants escalation?</li></ul></div><div class="card gold"><h3>Facilitator scoring</h3><p>0 = aspiration only. 1 = outcome named. 2 = outcome plus quality/evidence. 3 = full CLEAR agreement, with realistic authority and rhythm.</p></div></div><div class="callout"><b>Transfer assignment:</b> Before the next session, test one CLEAR standard in a real conversation. Bring the employee’s clarifying questions to the cohort. Questions reveal where the original standard still relied on manager assumptions.</div></section><section class="page"><div class="eyebrow">THE HIGH-PERFORMANCE LEADERSHIP SYSTEM</div><h2>Module 3: Create Commitment, Not Compliance</h2><p>Managers need to set direction. Yet direction alone rarely creates ownership. Commitment comes when people understand the why, contribute their view of reality, know their authority, and can state the agreement in their own words.</p><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Manager move</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr><tr><td>1. State outcome and why</td><td>“We need accurate proposal handoffs because customers experience our teams as one company.”</td><td>Connects the standard to purpose and customer impact.</td></tr><tr><td>2. Ask for current reality</td><td>“What would make this difficult this week?”</td><td>Surfaces constraints before they become excuses or surprises.</td></tr><tr><td>3. Agree standard and authority</td><td>“You can choose the handoff format; legal exceptions come to me.”</td><td>Creates practical autonomy.</td></tr><tr><td>4. Ask for a playback</td><td>“How are you taking this forward?”</td><td>Tests shared understanding without a quiz.</td></tr><tr><td>5. Set rhythm and support</td><td>“What support do you need, and when will we review the first two?”</td><td>Converts intention into a managed commitment.</td></tr></table><div class="callout"><b>Facilitator demonstration:</b> Role-play a 90-second compliance conversation, then repeat it as a commitment conversation. Ask observers what changed in employee voice, risk discovery, and clarity.</div><div class="exercise"><div class="label">Manager preparation</div><p>For a live expectation conversation: outcome __________; why it matters __________; question I will ask __________; decision rights __________; evidence/date __________.</p></div></section><section class="page"><div class="eyebrow">THE HIGH-PERFORMANCE LEADERSHIP SYSTEM</div><h2>Sample Dialogue: The Commitment Conversation</h2><p><b>Context:</b> Marcus, a team lead, needs Elena to improve weekly account-risk updates. The old instruction was “keep the account team in the loop.”</p><table><tr><th>Speaker</th><th>Dialogue</th><th>Leadership move</th></tr><tr><td>Marcus</td><td>“Elena, I want to set a clearer standard for account risks. When we learn about a risk late, the customer gets mixed messages. By 3 p.m. each Thursday, I need a short update on every amber or red account: risk, owner, next action, and any decision needed.”</td><td>States outcome, quality, and why.</td></tr><tr><td>Elena</td><td>“Some owners do not respond by then. I end up chasing them.”</td><td>Brings constraint into the open.</td></tr><tr><td>Marcus</td><td>“What would let you move it without waiting indefinitely?”</td><td>Uses curiosity, not blame.</td></tr><tr><td>Elena</td><td>“If I can mark the owner as unconfirmed and tag their lead by noon, I can still send the update.”</td><td>Proposes a workable authority path.</td></tr><tr><td>Marcus</td><td>“Yes. Do that. You decide the status based on evidence; tag me only if an executive customer decision is needed. Walk me through how you will run this Thursday.”</td><td>Names authority and asks playback.</td></tr><tr><td>Elena</td><td>“I will request inputs Tuesday, send a noon reminder Thursday, then publish the update at 3 p.m. with unconfirmed items visible.”</td><td>Commits to a rhythm.</td></tr></table><div class="callout"><b>Observer checklist:</b> Did the manager explain why, elicit reality, define authority, seek playback, and agree a review? Which phrase protected both standards and warmth?</div></section><section class="page"><div class="eyebrow">THE HIGH-PERFORMANCE LEADERSHIP SYSTEM</div><h2>Module 4: Coach the Gap Early with FACT</h2><p>Early correction is kinder and more effective than accumulated frustration. FACT keeps the manager out of labels and the employee out of defensiveness.</p><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Manager behavior</th><th>Language stem</th></tr><tr><td>F · Facts</td><td>Describe observable behavior and impact, not a character judgment.</td><td>“Three client updates were sent after the agreed date.”</td></tr><tr><td>A · Ask</td><td>Invite the person’s perspective before assuming cause.</td><td>“What got in the way?”</td></tr><tr><td>C · Clarify</td><td>Restate the standard and why it matters.</td><td>“The standard is same-day updates because silence creates avoidable uncertainty.”</td></tr><tr><td>T · Test</td><td>Agree next action, support, and check-in.</td><td>“What will change this week, what support is needed, and when will we review?”</td></tr></table><div class="grid"><div class="card"><h3>FACT is not soft</h3><p>It is specific, timely, and follows through. Curiosity does not erase the standard. It helps a manager distinguish ambiguity, capability, capacity, or will before choosing the right intervention.</p></div><div class="card gold"><h3>FACT is not a script</h3><p>Use natural language. A manager who mechanically asks questions while avoiding the consequence still teaches that standards are optional.</p></div></div><p><b>Escalation logic:</b> First confirm the agreement and remove barriers. If the same gap persists, state the impact, reset the corrective action, explain consequences consistent with policy, document the agreement, and follow up.</p><span class="small">Evidence base: Edmondson (1999); Locke & Latham (2002); Hattie & Timperley (2007); Deci, Olafsen & Ryan (2017); Gallup (2024). Full references at the end.</span></section><section class="page"><div class="eyebrow">THE HIGH-PERFORMANCE LEADERSHIP SYSTEM</div><h2>Coaching Practice Lab and Observer Rubric</h2><p><b>Scenario:</b> A project coordinator missed two internal handoffs and did not flag the risk until the deadline day. The employee says, “I was overwhelmed and did not want to bother anyone.” Run a five-minute conversation, then rotate.</p><table><tr><th>Observer criterion</th><th>0 = absent</th><th>1 = partial</th><th>2 = strong</th></tr><tr><td>Facts</td><td>Uses labels or hearsay</td><td>Some examples, unclear impact</td><td>Specific observable facts and impact</td></tr><tr><td>Ask</td><td>Assumes motive</td><td>Asks one closed question</td><td>Invites perspective and listens</td></tr><tr><td>Clarify</td><td>Vague standard</td><td>Standard stated without why</td><td>Clear standard, rationale, and authority</td></tr><tr><td>Test</td><td>No next step</td><td>Action but no review</td><td>Action, support, evidence, and date</td></tr><tr><td>Warmth</td><td>Cold or rescuing</td><td>Respectful but passive</td><td>Respectful, candid, and confident</td></tr><tr><td>Follow-through</td><td>Avoids consequence</td><td>Hints at consequence</td><td>Names proportional next step/policy path</td></tr></table><div class="exercise"><div class="label">Debrief questions</div><p>What did the manager learn only because they asked? Which part of the agreement was missing before the gap? What response would reduce the likelihood of late surprise next time?</p><p><b>Second round twist:</b> The employee has the skill and resources, but repeats the behavior after two clear agreements. Practice a firmer accountability conversation without changing the tone to punishment.</p></div></section><section class="page"><div class="eyebrow">THE HIGH-PERFORMANCE LEADERSHIP SYSTEM</div><h2>Case Study 2: The Product Launch That Slipped</h2><p><b>Situation.</b> A product team missed an internal launch by three weeks. The launch manager, Sam, says every workstream lead reported “on track” until the final ten days. In the retrospective, people blame a designer who changed the onboarding flow late. The designer says she had raised usability concerns twice but felt the group wanted momentum.</p><table><tr><th>Evidence</th><th>What it suggests</th></tr><tr><td>Status reports use green/amber/red but require no written risk, owner, or decision needed.</td><td>Colors conceal different realities; no evidence standard.</td></tr><tr><td>The weekly meeting spends 35 minutes on updates and five minutes on blockers.</td><td>Rhythm favors reporting over risk resolution.</td></tr><tr><td>Workstream leads ask Sam for approval on routine tradeoffs.</td><td>Authority is centralized; owners delay decisions.</td></tr><tr><td>The team praised “moving fast” but did not recognize early risk escalation.</td><td>Incentives may favor optimism over candor.</td></tr></table><div class="exercise"><div class="label">Leadership team task</div><p>Design a 20-minute weekly operating rhythm that surfaces risk early. Decide: What must each owner bring? What can they decide? What needs a group decision? How will early escalation be recognized?</p></div><div class="callout"><b>Facilitator note:</b> Participants often propose more tracking. Redirect them to fewer, sharper signals: commitment, evidence, risk, decision, owner, date.</div></section><section class="page"><div class="eyebrow">THE HIGH-PERFORMANCE LEADERSHIP SYSTEM</div><h2>Accountability Without Drama</h2><p>Accountability is an agreement plus a follow-through process. It is not a surprise, a personality judgment, a public performance, or a manager’s release valve for frustration.</p><table><tr><th>Sequence</th><th>What to do</th><th>Example language</th></tr><tr><td>1. Reconfirm</td><td>Name the prior agreement.</td><td>“Last Tuesday we agreed each account would have a next action by Friday.”</td></tr><tr><td>2. Describe result</td><td>State observed outcome and impact.</td><td>“Six of twelve did not. That left the renewal team unable to plan.”</td></tr><tr><td>3. Ask</td><td>Learn what changed or was missed.</td><td>“What happened between the agreement and Friday?”</td></tr><tr><td>4. Correct</td><td>Set action, support, and date.</td><td>“Today we will complete the six; you will use the daily tracker through next Friday.”</td></tr><tr><td>5. Explain consequence</td><td>Where appropriate, explain the next formal step consistent with policy.</td><td>“If this repeats after the support we have agreed, we will move to our formal process.”</td></tr><tr><td>6. Document/follow up</td><td>Record the agreement and check it.</td><td>“I will send the summary; we meet Wednesday at 10.”</td></tr></table><div class="callout"><b>Guardrails:</b> Stay private, factual, timely, and proportionate. Distinguish performance management from conduct, and follow organizational HR and legal policy. Do not promise outcomes or impose consequences outside your authority.</div><p class="quote">The credible manager does not surprise people with standards, and does not abandon standards when the conversation gets uncomfortable.</p></section><section class="page"><div class="eyebrow">THE HIGH-PERFORMANCE LEADERSHIP SYSTEM</div><h2>Sample Dialogue: Firm, Fair, and Forward</h2><p><b>Context:</b> After two FACT conversations, Jonah again failed to complete a required quality check. His manager, Mei, must be direct without shaming.</p><div class="card"><p><b>Mei:</b> “Jonah, we agreed on September 8 that every release would include the quality check before handoff. The last two releases went forward without it, and we found three defects after deployment. Help me understand what changed.”</p><p><b>Jonah:</b> “The deadline moved and I thought getting it out was more important.”</p><p><b>Mei:</b> “I understand the deadline pressure. The quality check is not optional; it is part of a release. When timing conflicts, your authority is to pause the handoff and bring the tradeoff to me. For the next four releases, use the checklist and send it with the handoff. We will review after each one. If the agreed standard is missed again, we will move to the formal performance process. What is your understanding of the plan?”</p><p><b>Jonah:</b> “I will not choose speed over the check. If I see a conflict, I will flag it before the handoff.”</p></div><table><tr><th>What Mei did</th><th>Why it works</th></tr><tr><td>Referenced a prior agreement and facts</td><td>No surprise; discussion starts from shared reality.</td></tr><tr><td>Acknowledged pressure without excusing the gap</td><td>Maintains warmth and standard simultaneously.</td></tr><tr><td>Clarified authority at the point of tradeoff</td><td>Removes a likely repeat cause.</td></tr><tr><td>Set temporary evidence and review rhythm</td><td>Creates support and accountability.</td></tr><tr><td>Named the next consequence calmly</td><td>Makes follow-through credible, not threatening.</td></tr></table><div class="exercise"><div class="label">Adapt</div><p>Rewrite Mei’s first two sentences for one real performance gap you need to address. Remove labels such as “careless,” “lazy,” or “not committed.”</p></div></section><section class="page"><div class="eyebrow">THE HIGH-PERFORMANCE LEADERSHIP SYSTEM</div><h2>Module 5: Delegate for Ownership</h2><p>Delegation fails when managers hand over tasks but retain outcomes, decisions, and learning. Ownership grows when people understand the result, have meaningful control, receive appropriate guardrails, and can seek help without surrendering the work.</p><table><tr><th>Delegation element</th><th>Define it explicitly</th><th>Common failure</th></tr><tr><td>Outcome</td><td>What result must be true?</td><td>Giving a list of tasks with no purpose.</td></tr><tr><td>Guardrails</td><td>What must not be compromised?</td><td>“Use your judgment” with hidden constraints.</td></tr><tr><td>Authority</td><td>What decisions belong to the owner?</td><td>Requiring approval for routine choices.</td></tr><tr><td>Resources</td><td>What time, people, data, or budget are available?</td><td>Assigning work without capacity.</td></tr><tr><td>Risks</td><td>What warrants escalation, and when?</td><td>Only hearing about a problem when it is urgent.</td></tr><tr><td>Review date</td><td>When will we learn, adjust, or close?</td><td>Checking constantly or disappearing entirely.</td></tr><tr><td>Definition of done</td><td>What evidence confirms completion?</td><td>Arguments about whether work is finished.</td></tr></table><div class="callout"><b>Manager mindset shift:</b> If you solve every hard problem, you create relief today and dependence tomorrow. If you delegate without clarity or support, you create anxiety and avoidable failure. Good delegation is a development intervention and an operating agreement.</div><div class="exercise"><div class="label">Delegation brief</div><p>Pick one task you are tempted to keep. What outcome could another person own? What decision rights would make the delegation real?</p></div></section><section class="page"><div class="eyebrow">THE HIGH-PERFORMANCE LEADERSHIP SYSTEM</div><h2>Worksheet: Delegation Brief</h2><p>Use this brief before a delegation conversation. It is designed to make ownership visible without over-controlling the method.</p><table><tr><th>Element</th><th>Agreement</th></tr><tr><td>Outcome and why it matters</td><td><br><br></td></tr><tr><td>Definition of done / evidence</td><td><br><br></td></tr><tr><td>Non-negotiable guardrails</td><td><br><br></td></tr><tr><td>Decisions the owner can make</td><td><br><br></td></tr><tr><td>Decisions to escalate and trigger</td><td><br><br></td></tr><tr><td>Resources, stakeholders, and constraints</td><td><br><br></td></tr><tr><td>First review date and rhythm</td><td><br><br></td></tr><tr><td>Capability stretch: what will this person learn?</td><td><br><br></td></tr></table><div class="grid"><div class="card"><h3>Manager close</h3><p>“What do you understand the outcome to be? Which decisions will you make? Where do you see risk? What support would help without taking this back from you?”</p></div><div class="card gold"><h3>Owner close</h3><p>“I will know I am done when ____. I will escalate if ____. Our first review is ____.”</p></div></div></section><section class="page"><div class="eyebrow">THE HIGH-PERFORMANCE LEADERSHIP SYSTEM</div><h2>Recognition: Reinforce What You Want Repeated</h2><p>Recognition is not a substitute for feedback or pay. It is a management signal: it tells the team which behaviors make standards real. Generic praise can feel pleasant but teaches little. Specific recognition connects behavior, impact, and value.</p><table><tr><th>Weak recognition</th><th>Specific recognition</th><th>What it reinforces</th></tr><tr><td>“Great job.”</td><td>“You raised the supplier risk on Tuesday, brought two options, and protected Friday’s customer commitment.”</td><td>Early escalation and solution ownership.</td></tr><tr><td>“Thanks for working hard.”</td><td>“Your handoff included the decision log and customer context; the receiving team started without rework.”</td><td>Quality and cross-team empathy.</td></tr><tr><td>“You are a star.”</td><td>“You coached Mia through the analysis instead of taking it back. She now owns the next review.”</td><td>Development and delegation.</td></tr></table><div class="callout"><b>Weekly ritual:</b> In the team meeting, recognize one observable behavior aligned to the standard and one example of constructive risk escalation. Keep it brief, factual, and inclusive. Do not turn recognition into a popularity contest.</div><div class="exercise"><div class="label">Practice</div><p>Write one sentence of recognition for a behavior you want repeated this month. Include: behavior __________, impact __________, standard/value __________.</p><div class="line"></div></div><p class="small">Facilitator note: Ask participants how they might recognize quiet, invisible coordination work without rewarding overwork or individual heroics.</p></section><section class="page"><div class="eyebrow">THE HIGH-PERFORMANCE LEADERSHIP SYSTEM</div><h2>Module 6: Install the Weekly Operating Rhythm</h2><p>Routines convert good leadership intent into a system. The goal is not more meetings. It is a short cadence where priorities, commitments, risks, decisions, and learning are visible before they become emergencies.</p><table><tr><th>Cadence</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Agenda / evidence</th><th>Leader behavior</th></tr><tr><td>Daily or twice-weekly, 10–15 min</td><td>Coordinate work and remove immediate blockers</td><td>Priority shifts; blocked work; owner/date</td><td>Keep it operational; resolve offline where possible.</td></tr><tr><td>Weekly team rhythm, 30 min</td><td>Review commitments, quality, risk, and recognition</td><td>Scorecard; commitments; risks; decisions; win/learning</td><td>Ask owners to speak first; make decisions visible.</td></tr><tr><td>1:1 coaching, 25–40 min</td><td>Develop capability and address gaps early</td><td>Employee agenda; outcomes; FACT conversation; support</td><td>Coach, do not turn it into a status interrogation.</td></tr><tr><td>Monthly improvement review, 45–60 min</td><td>Spot recurring friction and adjust the system</td><td>Trend data; root causes; one improvement experiment</td><td>Focus on process and capability, not blame.</td></tr></table><div class="diagram"><h3>Weekly rhythm flow</h3><p><b>1. Outcomes:</b> What moved? → <b>2. Commitments:</b> What is due next? → <b>3. Risks:</b> What could prevent it? → <b>4. Decisions:</b> Who decides by when? → <b>5. Learning:</b> What standard or process changes? → <b>6. Recognition:</b> What behavior should repeat?</p></div><p>Use a visible board or simple shared document. The format matters less than the discipline: owner, evidence, next action, date.</p></section><section class="page"><div class="eyebrow">THE HIGH-PERFORMANCE LEADERSHIP SYSTEM</div><h2>Team Scorecard and Meeting Template</h2><p>A team scorecard should be small enough to use, broad enough to prevent gaming one metric at the expense of the whole system. Select measures relevant to the team’s purpose.</p><table><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Example measure</th><th>Current</th><th>Target</th><th>Owner / next action</th></tr><tr><td>Outcome</td><td>On-time delivery or resolved work</td><td>____</td><td>____</td><td>____</td></tr><tr><td>Quality</td><td>First-pass acceptance; defect or rework rate</td><td>____</td><td>____</td><td>____</td></tr><tr><td>Customer</td><td>Response time; satisfaction; renewal risk</td><td>____</td><td>____</td><td>____</td></tr><tr><td>Flow</td><td>Blocked-work age; handoff delay</td><td>____</td><td>____</td><td>____</td></tr><tr><td>Capability</td><td>Practice completion; cross-training progress</td><td>____</td><td>____</td><td>____</td></tr><tr><td>Team health</td><td>Clarity / safety pulse</td><td>____</td><td>____</td><td>____</td></tr></table><div class="grid"><div class="card"><h3>30-minute agenda</h3><ol class="small"><li>2 min: purpose and scorecard</li><li>8 min: commitments and evidence</li><li>8 min: risks and decisions</li><li>6 min: improvement experiment</li><li>3 min: recognition</li><li>3 min: owner/date recap</li></ol></div><div class="card gold"><h3>Manager prompts</h3><p>“What is the evidence?” “What decision belongs with you?” “What risk are we not naming?” “What will be different by our next review?”</p></div></div><div class="callout"><b>Do not use the scorecard to punish disclosure.</b> If a metric turns red, reward the team for identifying the cause early and agreeing a credible recovery action.</div></section><section class="page"><div class="eyebrow">THE HIGH-PERFORMANCE LEADERSHIP SYSTEM</div><h2>30-Day Manager Implementation Calendar</h2><p>The first month creates momentum by applying one tool each week to real work. Participants should not attempt to transform every process at once.</p><table><tr><th>Week</th><th>Practice</th><th>Manager actions</th><th>Evidence to bring to cohort</th></tr><tr><td>1 · Clarify</td><td>One CLEAR standard</td><td>Choose a recurring friction; define outcome, quality, evidence, authority, and rhythm; run one commitment conversation.</td><td>The written standard plus one employee question.</td></tr><tr><td>2 · Commit</td><td>Two ownership conversations</td><td>Use playback; define decisions and escalation triggers; revise language based on questions.</td><td>Two short notes: agreement, risk, support, review date.</td></tr><tr><td>3 · Coach</td><td>One FACT conversation</td><td>Address a live performance gap within 48 hours; distinguish facts from labels; agree next action.</td><td>What you learned, action, and evidence date.</td></tr><tr><td>4 · Systemize</td><td>One weekly rhythm</td><td>Install a 30-minute team cadence; add commitments, risks, decisions, and recognition.</td><td>Scorecard photo/export and one change to make next week.</td></tr></table><div class="exercise"><div class="label">Daily 5-minute reflection</div><p>What standard did I make clearer today? Where did I ask before telling? What did I take back that another person could own? What evidence did we agree? Who did I recognize specifically?</p></div><div class="callout"><b>Accountability partner:</b> Pair managers. Each sends a Friday message with one practice completed, one learning, and next week’s action. The partner asks one question rather than offering a solution.</div></section><section class="page"><div class="eyebrow">THE HIGH-PERFORMANCE LEADERSHIP SYSTEM</div><h2>90-Day Reinforcement and Measurement Plan</h2><p>Training changes language. Reinforcement changes routines. This plan creates repeated practice, observable evidence, and buyer-level visibility into whether the system is taking hold.</p><table><tr><th>Period</th><th>Leader actions</th><th>Sponsor actions</th><th>Measures</th></tr><tr><td>Days 0–30</td><td>Complete calendar; use tools in live work; join peer practice.</td><td>Confirm priority friction and baseline; remove structural blockers.</td><td>Practice completion; clarity pulse; initial operational baseline.</td></tr><tr><td>Days 31–60</td><td>Run coaching circles; review pressure patterns; improve weekly rhythm.</td><td>Review trends with manager cohort; recognize early wins.</td><td>On-time/quality trends; early-risk rate; manager confidence.</td></tr><tr><td>Days 61–90</td><td>Teach a peer; sustain scorecard; choose next capability gap.</td><td>Decide scale, support, or policy/process changes.</td><td>30/60/90 comparison; employee clarity/safety pulse; relevant customer measure.</td></tr></table><div class="diagram"><h3>Measurement logic</h3><p><b>Leading indicators:</b> CLEAR agreements, coaching conversations, delegation briefs, meeting discipline → <b>Behavior indicators:</b> clarity, early escalation, decision ownership → <b>Operating outcomes:</b> delivery, quality, customer impact, capacity.</p></div><div class="callout"><b>Interpret with care:</b> Do not claim causation from one short period. Look for a consistent pattern: adoption of manager practices, employee experience shifts, then improvement in the operational measures the team can influence.</div></section><section class="page"><div class="eyebrow">THE HIGH-PERFORMANCE LEADERSHIP SYSTEM</div><h2>Facilitator Guide: Flagship One-Day Workshop</h2><p><b>Audience:</b> 20–30 frontline managers and team leads. <b>Room:</b> tables of four, visible wall space, projected timer, printed workbook. <b>Outcome:</b> each participant leaves with one real leadership-system experiment and a peer accountability partner.</p><table><tr><th>Time</th><th>Segment</th><th>Method</th><th>Facilitator outcome</th></tr><tr><td>09:00</td><td>Open and contract</td><td>Buyer context, norms, friction prompt</td><td>Establish relevance and psychological safety.</td></tr><tr><td>09:25</td><td>Standards × Warmth</td><td>Teach + pressure-pattern diagnostic</td><td>Name defaults without judgment.</td></tr><tr><td>10:20</td><td>Friction map</td><td>Individual work + triads</td><td>Turn vague concerns into system diagnosis.</td></tr><tr><td>11:15</td><td>CLEAR standards</td><td>Teach + rewrite lab</td><td>Produce one executable standard.</td></tr><tr><td>13:00</td><td>Commitment conversations</td><td>Demo + paired practice</td><td>Build ownership language.</td></tr><tr><td>14:15</td><td>FACT coaching</td><td>Teach + role-play rubric</td><td>Practice timely correction.</td></tr><tr><td>15:30</td><td>Accountability and delegation</td><td>Case + dialogue practice</td><td>Increase firmness and decision ownership.</td></tr><tr><td>16:30</td><td>Operating rhythm and 30-day plan</td><td>Scorecard build + commitments</td><td>Convert insight into repeatable routines.</td></tr><tr><td>17:00</td><td>Close</td><td>Partner commitment and sponsor message</td><td>Lock in next action and support.</td></tr></table><div class="callout"><b>Room norm:</b> “Discuss real work, preserve confidentiality, and challenge the system before judging the person.” Keep examples anonymized.</div></section><section class="page"><div class="eyebrow">THE HIGH-PERFORMANCE LEADERSHIP SYSTEM</div><h2>Facilitator Notes: Practice, Inclusion, and Recovery</h2><div class="two"><p><b>Before delivery:</b> Ask the buyer for 2–3 anonymized friction examples, current language for performance management, and relevant operating measures. Tailor examples, but do not invent policies or promise formal HR guidance.</p><p><b>When participants say “we do not have time”:</b> Acknowledge the constraint. Ask what rework, escalation, or manager rescue currently consumes. Position the rhythm as a replacement for low-value status traffic, not an additive bureaucracy.</p><p><b>When participants want scripts:</b> Give language stems, then insist on natural phrasing. The manager must sound credible in their environment.</p><p><b>When someone describes a sensitive performance issue:</b> Redirect to facts and process. Remind the group to follow local policy and seek HR support where required.</p><p><b>Inclusion practice:</b> Avoid treating directness as one cultural style. Define directness as clear, respectful, evidence-based communication. Invite multiple ways to express warmth and challenge.</p><p><b>Energy recovery:</b> After dense teaching, use pair rehearsals of 90 seconds each. Give observers one narrow criterion. Debrief patterns, not individual competence.</p></div><table><tr><th>Moment</th><th>Facilitator question</th><th>Listen for</th></tr><tr><td>Vague diagnosis</td><td>“What would we observe if that were true?”</td><td>Labels becoming facts.</td></tr><tr><td>Low ownership</td><td>“What decision could move closer to the work?”</td><td>Hidden approval loops.</td></tr><tr><td>Avoided feedback</td><td>“What is the cost of waiting another week?”</td><td>Fear of conflict, unclear standard.</td></tr><tr><td>Over-control</td><td>“What evidence would let you step back safely?”</td><td>Manager need for certainty.</td></tr></table></section><section class="page"><div class="eyebrow">THE HIGH-PERFORMANCE LEADERSHIP SYSTEM</div><h2>Virtual Delivery Design: Four 90-Minute Sessions</h2><p>Virtual delivery works when it reduces lecture and protects rehearsal. Use a shared digital workbook, breakout pairs, visible timer, and a live commitment tracker.</p><table><tr><th>Session</th><th>Core content</th><th>Live practice</th><th>Between-session assignment</th></tr><tr><td>1 · Diagnose and clarify</td><td>Standards × Warmth; Friction Map; CLEAR</td><td>Rewrite one standard; peer test</td><td>Run one CLEAR conversation.</td></tr><tr><td>2 · Create commitment</td><td>Conversation sequence; authority; playback</td><td>90-second expectation conversation</td><td>Document two commitments and questions received.</td></tr><tr><td>3 · Coach and follow through</td><td>FACT; accountability guardrails</td><td>Role-play with observer rubric</td><td>Use FACT on one live, timely gap.</td></tr><tr><td>4 · Systemize</td><td>Delegation; scorecard; 30-day plan</td><td>Build weekly rhythm; partner commitments</td><td>Run first rhythm and bring evidence.</td></tr></table><div class="grid"><div class="card"><h3>Virtual facilitation rules</h3><ul><li>Teach no more than 12 minutes without interaction.</li><li>Use a shared artifact for every breakout.</li><li>Give roles: manager, employee, observer.</li><li>Harvest one pattern, not every answer.</li></ul></div><div class="card gold"><h3>Technical fallback</h3><p>If breakouts fail, use chat-based paired prompts and invite two volunteers to model. Keep the practice objective; do not fill the time with additional slides.</p></div></div></section><section class="page"><div class="eyebrow">THE HIGH-PERFORMANCE LEADERSHIP SYSTEM</div><h2>Buyer ROI Model: Build a Credible Case</h2><p>Use this model to estimate value, not to make inflated promises. Select one or two operational levers where the buyer has reliable baseline data. Agree assumptions with the buyer and show sensitivity.</p><table><tr><th>Value lever</th><th>Calculation</th><th>Illustrative input</th></tr><tr><td>Reduced rework</td><td>Hours avoided per month × loaded hourly cost × capture rate</td><td>80 hrs × $55 × 50% = $2,200/month</td></tr><tr><td>Fewer escalations</td><td>Escalations avoided × average manager/specialist hours × cost</td><td>12 × 2.5 hrs × $70 = $2,100/month</td></tr><tr><td>Faster delivery</td><td>Work items accelerated × contribution or capacity value</td><td>Use buyer-validated unit value</td></tr><tr><td>Lower regrettable turnover</td><td>Avoided exits × replacement-cost assumption</td><td>Use conservative finance/HR estimate</td></tr><tr><td>Manager capacity</td><td>Hours no longer spent rescuing × loaded cost</td><td>20 hrs × $75 = $1,500/month</td></tr></table><div class="callout"><b>Illustrative annualized value:</b> If a pilot captures $2,200 in rework reduction, $2,100 in escalation reduction, and $1,500 in manager capacity per month, the annualized gross value is <b>$69,600</b> before any retention or revenue effects. Compare with program and implementation cost; do not double-count overlapping benefits.</div><div class="exercise"><div class="label">ROI formula</div><p>Net benefit = validated annual benefit − total program cost. ROI % = (net benefit ÷ total program cost) × 100. Payback months = total program cost ÷ monthly validated benefit.</p></div></section><section class="page"><div class="eyebrow">THE HIGH-PERFORMANCE LEADERSHIP SYSTEM</div><h2>Buyer Business Case and Pilot Design</h2><p><b>Recommended pilot:</b> one manager cohort in a team with a visible operating problem and enough stability to measure. Avoid a pilot during a major reorganization unless the buyer explicitly wants leadership support for that transition.</p><table><tr><th>Design decision</th><th>Recommendation</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Cohort</td><td>15–30 managers, ideally connected to a common operating context</td><td>Creates peer practice and common measures.</td></tr><tr><td>Baseline</td><td>Capture 4 weeks of relevant performance data plus clarity/safety pulse</td><td>Creates a credible comparison point.</td></tr><tr><td>Sponsor role</td><td>Open the program, reinforce priorities, review 30/60/90 trends</td><td>Signals that practice matters beyond the workshop.</td></tr><tr><td>Manager time</td><td>One day or four 90-minute sessions plus weekly 20–30 minutes of application</td><td>Protects transfer to live work.</td></tr><tr><td>Success threshold</td><td>Agree 2 practice measures + 1–2 operating measures in advance</td><td>Prevents retrospective goal shifting.</td></tr></table><div class="grid"><div class="card"><h3>Executive sponsor message</h3><p>“We are not asking managers to add performance pressure. We are asking them to create more clarity, earlier learning, and more ownership. We will measure the routines, not just attend the training.”</p></div><div class="card gold"><h3>Discovery questions</h3><p>Which teams feel the cost most? What would improve if risks were surfaced earlier? Which management behavior currently creates the biggest bottleneck? What data exists?</p></div></div></section><section class="page"><div class="eyebrow">THE HIGH-PERFORMANCE LEADERSHIP SYSTEM</div><h2>Microlearning Series: Learning Architecture</h2><p>Each microlearning video solves one recognizable manager problem, teaches one model, shows one realistic application, and ends with a one-week behavior challenge. Keep sales messaging outside the teaching core.</p><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Working title</th><th>Length</th><th>Core takeaway</th><th>Downloadable asset</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Why Good Teams Underperform</td><td>7 min</td><td>Inconsistency is often a leadership-system problem.</td><td>Friction Map</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Standards × Warmth</td><td>8 min</td><td>Demand and respect reinforce each other.</td><td>Self-assessment</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Make Expectations CLEAR</td><td>9 min</td><td>Good performance must be observable.</td><td>CLEAR template</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Commitment, Not Compliance</td><td>8 min</td><td>Ownership starts with dialogue and authority.</td><td>Conversation guide</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Coach the Gap Early</td><td>9 min</td><td>FACT corrects without blame.</td><td>Prompt card</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Accountability Without Drama</td><td>8 min</td><td>Follow-through is fair and specific.</td><td>Checklist</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Delegate for Ownership</td><td>7 min</td><td>Delegate outcomes, authority, and guardrails.</td><td>Delegation brief</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Recognize What You Want Repeated</td><td>6 min</td><td>Specific recognition shapes norms.</td><td>Recognition examples</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>The Weekly Operating Rhythm</td><td>8 min</td><td>Routines make performance repeatable.</td><td>Agenda + scorecard</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Your 30-Day Leadership Reset</td><td>7 min</td><td>Apply the system to one real challenge.</td><td>Action plan</td></tr></table><div class="callout"><b>Learning sequence:</b> Diagnose → clarify → commit → coach → follow through → delegate → reinforce → systemize. Do not rearrange the series into a collection of tips; the value is the connected operating system.</div></section><section class="page"><div class="eyebrow">THE HIGH-PERFORMANCE LEADERSHIP SYSTEM</div><h2>Video Production Guide: From Script to Shoot</h2><p><b>Creative principle:</b> Open with a recognizable manager moment, not a definition. Show the cost, teach one model, demonstrate the behavior, then issue a practice challenge.</p><table><tr><th>Production element</th><th>Guidance</th><th>Quality check</th></tr><tr><td>Opening 0:00–0:30</td><td>Use a specific tension: late surprise, vague handover, manager rescue.</td><td>Viewer can recognize the situation in 10 seconds.</td></tr><tr><td>Teach 0:30–3:00</td><td>Use one model only; animate the 2×2, flow, or checklist.</td><td>No concept pile-up; plain-language explanation.</td></tr><tr><td>Demonstration 3:00–5:30</td><td>Show a bad version briefly, then an improved conversation.</td><td>Dialogue sounds natural; facts not labels.</td></tr><tr><td>Application 5:30–7:00</td><td>Walk through one realistic example and downloadable asset.</td><td>Viewer sees what to do next.</td></tr><tr><td>Challenge + next step</td><td>Give one behavior for the coming week; preview next video.</td><td>Challenge is observable and limited.</td></tr></table><div class="grid"><div class="card"><h3>Visual system</h3><p>Use navy (#093A4D), teal (#0D5D68), and gold (#D2A247). Favor simple diagrams, on-screen phrases, and document close-ups. Avoid stock imagery that implies performance rather than showing the manager action.</p></div><div class="card gold"><h3>Talent direction</h3><p>Speak as a practical manager coach, not a lecturer. Pause after questions. Make eye-line and voice warm; deliver standards plainly. Record alternate takes of each dialogue line for editing flexibility.</p></div></div></section><section class="page"><div class="eyebrow">THE HIGH-PERFORMANCE LEADERSHIP SYSTEM</div><h2>Video Script Template and Asset Checklist</h2><p>Use this repeatable production template for each episode.</p><table><tr><th>Beat</th><th>Script prompt</th><th>Production asset</th></tr><tr><td>Hook</td><td>“Have you ever discovered a problem only when it was too late to fix easily?”</td><td>B-roll: queue, missed handoff, status board.</td></tr><tr><td>Name the problem</td><td>“The issue may not be effort. It may be an unclear leadership system.”</td><td>Title card and simple friction diagram.</td></tr><tr><td>Teach one model</td><td>Define the model in under 90 seconds.</td><td>Animated model; one highlighted term at a time.</td></tr><tr><td>Show it</td><td>Manager/employee scene: poor version then improved version.</td><td>Two-camera dialogue; captions.</td></tr><tr><td>Apply it</td><td>Work one realistic example through the tool.</td><td>Screen capture or annotated worksheet.</td></tr><tr><td>Challenge</td><td>“This week, use this on one recurring friction.”</td><td>Download CTA; next-episode card.</td></tr></table><div class="grid"><div class="card"><h3>Pre-production checklist</h3><ul class="check"><li>Buyer-approved examples and terminology</li><li>Script reviewed for one clear takeaway</li><li>Downloadable tool ready and branded</li><li>Accessibility: captions, contrast, readable type</li></ul></div><div class="card gold"><h3>Post-production checklist</h3><ul class="check"><li>Captions and transcript verified</li><li>Model labels match workbook language</li><li>Practice challenge is measurable</li><li>Links, assets, and next-video cue tested</li></ul></div></div></section><section class="page"><div class="eyebrow">THE HIGH-PERFORMANCE LEADERSHIP SYSTEM</div><h2>Product Assets, Sales Positioning, and Launch Copy</h2><div class="grid"><div><h3>One-sentence positioning</h3><p>A practical leadership system that helps managers raise standards, build trust, and create ownership without relying on fear, micromanagement, or heroics.</p><h3>Buyer-facing description</h3><p>When performance is inconsistent, most organizations respond with more meetings, escalation, or pressure. This program gives managers a better answer: clear standards, direct coaching, meaningful ownership, and operating rhythms that make great work easier to repeat.</p></div><div><h3>Included assets</h3><ul class="check"><li>Facilitator guide and learner workbook</li><li>Pressure-pattern self-assessment</li><li>Friction Map, CLEAR, FACT, and delegation tools</li><li>Role-play scenarios and observer rubrics</li><li>Scorecard, 30-day calendar, 90-day measurement plan</li><li>Buyer ROI model and video production kit</li></ul></div></div><table><tr><th>Channel</th><th>Message</th><th>Call to action</th></tr><tr><td>Landing page</td><td>Build managers your teams can trust and your business can rely on.</td><td>Book a buyer discovery conversation.</td></tr><tr><td>Sales call</td><td>Replace vague accountability with a system managers can use in their next conversation.</td><td>Choose a pilot friction and measurement plan.</td></tr><tr><td>Sponsor email</td><td>We will measure practice, clarity, and operating outcomes—not attendance alone.</td><td>Nominate the first cohort.</td></tr><tr><td>Video close</td><td>Try one behavior this week; download the tool and continue to the next module.</td><td>Access the workbook or cohort.</td></tr></table><div class="callout"><b>Ethical sales note:</b> Position the program as a capability and operating-system intervention. Do not guarantee revenue, retention, or culture outcomes that depend on conditions beyond the program’s scope.</div></section><section class="page"><div class="eyebrow">THE HIGH-PERFORMANCE LEADERSHIP SYSTEM</div><h2>Capstone: Your Leadership System Canvas</h2><p>Use this final canvas to connect the product to one real team condition. A strong plan is small, specific, and visible enough to review.</p><table><tr><th>Canvas element</th><th>Your decision</th></tr><tr><td>Priority friction to solve</td><td><br><br></td></tr><tr><td>CLEAR standard we will introduce</td><td><br><br></td></tr><tr><td>Commitment conversation: who / when</td><td><br><br></td></tr><tr><td>FACT conversation or coaching need</td><td><br><br></td></tr><tr><td>Delegation/decision right to shift</td><td><br><br></td></tr><tr><td>Weekly rhythm change</td><td><br><br></td></tr><tr><td>Leading evidence we will review</td><td><br><br></td></tr><tr><td>Outcome measure and 30-day target</td><td><br><br></td></tr><tr><td>Warmth behavior I will deliberately practice</td><td><br><br></td></tr><tr><td>Accountability partner and check-in date</td><td><br><br></td></tr></table><div class="callout"><b>Closing facilitator script:</b> “High performance is not created by pressure alone. It is created when people know what matters, have the support and authority to act, receive direct feedback, and experience consistent follow-through. Your job is to make that environment real every day.”</div></section><section class="page"><div class="eyebrow">THE HIGH-PERFORMANCE LEADERSHIP SYSTEM</div><h2>References and Source Notes</h2><p><b>Canonical product source:</b> <i>High_Performance_Leadership_Training_Product.md</i>, vault leadership and management media collection. The Standards × Warmth model is used as a practical learning framework; tailor examples, organizational policies, and measures to the buyer environment.</p><div class="two"><p>Deci, E. L., Olafsen, A. H., & Ryan, R. M. (2017). Self-determination theory in work organizations: The state of a science. <i>Annual Review of Organizational Psychology and Organizational Behavior, 4</i>, 19–43.</p><p>Edmondson, A. (1999). Psychological safety and learning behavior in work teams. <i>Administrative Science Quarterly, 44</i>(2), 350–383.</p><p>Gallup. (2024). <i>State of the Global Workplace: 2024 Report.</i> Gallup Press.</p><p>Hattie, J., & Timperley, H. (2007). The power of feedback. <i>Review of Educational Research, 77</i>(1), 81–112.</p><p>Locke, E. A., & Latham, G. P. (2002). Building a practically useful theory of goal setting and task motivation. <i>American Psychologist, 57</i>(9), 705–717.</p><p>Note for facilitators: These sources support the general learning design. They do not replace legal, HR, clinical, or employee-relations guidance. Adapt accountability sequences to local policy and seek appropriate expert support for formal performance matters.</p></div><div class="exercise"><div class="label">Final commitment</div><p>The leadership behavior I will practice first: ____________________________</p><p>The team outcome it will support: ____________________________</p><p>The evidence I will review on: ____________________________</p></div></section></body></html>